--- body ---
## Context

The permission allow-list had grown to 76 rules by accretion: every session that
hit a prompt left a rule behind, and nothing ever took one away. A permission
rule is not free. It is matched on every tool call, it is one more line the
model weighs, and a stale one grants nothing while reading like policy.

Third cut of the pre-port slimming, after 0881 and 0882.

## What was removed, and on what evidence

Nothing was removed for looking unused. Each of the 29 rules failed a checked
test:

**Named target no longer exists (9).** `Tracing Kieu` as a project directory,
`perso/Life manual/Heroes` and its three hero folders, and the
`chemin-de-voix/.claude/worktrees/harvest-corpus` worktree — all verified
absent on disk, not inferred.

**Literal from a completed operation (20).** No wildcard, so the rule can only
ever match that exact string again: the chemin-de-voix corpus harvest and its
clean-up, two import probes (`fpdf2`, `reportlab`), a `pkill` of one named
process, a `df` over an ssh host, three output-shaping filters, `fc-list`.

One `additionalDirectories` entry pointed at a directory that no longer exists
(`AEDIST-technical-report/tickets`).

## What was deliberately not done

**No rule was widened.** Several removed literals would be covered by one broad
rule over the chemin-de-voix checkout. Widening a permission is the author's
call, not a consequence of tidying, so this pass only narrows the file and never
the policy. Of the 29 removed, five carry a wildcard and every one of those five
names a directory that is gone.

**Same-matcher hook blocks were left unfolded.** `PreToolUse` holds four
separate blocks matching `Bash`, which could be one block with four hooks. On
paper that is a spelling change; in practice nothing here establishes whether a
deny from one hook short-circuits its block-mates the way it short-circuits
later blocks. The gain is three fewer lines in a file nobody reads by hand and
the risk is the guard layer, so it wants a fixture first. Filed as an
observation, not done.

## Relevant files

- `settings.shared.json` — the canonical copy; the live `settings.json` is
  git-ignored and re-aligned by hand
- `scripts/check-settings-drift.sh` — reports the gap until the live file is
  aligned

## Verification

- [x] JSON parses; 76 -> 47 rules, 4 -> 3 additional directories
- [x] No rule was added, and no removed wildcard rule names a live path
- [x] `make check` passes
- [ ] Live `settings.json` re-aligned (operator act, git-ignored file)

## Invariants

- No hook changes; no rule is widened; only rules whose subject is gone leave.

## Exit criteria

- The allow-list contains only rules that can still match something, and the
  drift check is clean once the live file is aligned.
